&gt; This might be the core contention. I don't know if using actual <em>capabilities</em> in a language would have problematically bad ergonomics. You'd probably be passing more arguments to functions. But haskell seems to manage ok despite needing to pass IO to functions that need it. <em>Capabilities</em> seem similarly inconvenient. I think I'd need to see it tried. I agree - I might need to try it myself.<p>You can try it now yourself in Haskell!  This is my effect <em>system</em>, based on <em>capabilities</em>: <a href="https://hackage.haskell.org/package/bluefin" rel="nofollow">https://hackage.haskell.org/package/bluefin</a><p>One of the common objections I hear to Bluefin is &quot;isn't it too inconvenient to pass around <em>capabilities</em> everywhere?&quot;. Perhaps surprisingly, no, I haven't found it remotely inconvenient. I find it liberating, actually.

